This TypeScript function from our checkout service is a pre-release cleanup target. It works, but it mutates its inputs, throws strings, and has no types:

```typescript
function processOrder(cart, user, promoCode) {
  var total = 0;
  for (var i = 0; i < cart.items.length; i++) {
    cart.items[i].price = cart.items[i].price * 1;
    if (cart.items[i].quantity < 0) {
      throw "bad quantity";
    }
    total = total + cart.items[i].price * cart.items[i].quantity;
  }
  if (promoCode) {
    if (promoCode == "SAVE10") {
      total = total * 0.9;
    } else if (promoCode == "FREE") {
      total = 0;
    } else {
      console.log("unknown promo, ignoring: " + promoCode);
    }
  }
  cart.items = [];
  user.lastOrderTotal = total;
  return { total: total, placedAt: new Date().toISOString() };
}
```

Refactor it into a clean, typed module for TypeScript 5.x in strict mode:

1. Define exported interfaces: CartItem, Cart, User, PromotionResult and Order.
2. Split the logic into small pure functions: validateCart, calculateSubtotal, applyPromotion and buildOrder, plus a top-level processOrder that composes them.
3. Replace the string throw with a typed CheckoutError class hierarchy (at minimum InvalidCartError and UnknownPromotionError). Unknown promo codes must be rejected, not silently ignored.
4. Do not mutate the input cart or user; return the new Order and the updated user as part of a result object.
5. Preserve the existing pricing behavior (SAVE10 = 10% off subtotal, FREE = total zero) and the ISO timestamp.

Reply with the complete refactored module in a single typescript code block, followed by a short "Changes" section of at most 150 words listing the behavioral decisions you made.
